# HARUTA — "The Scene We Didn't Plan"
# Route theme: choosing what you genuinely want instead of "anything is fine".
# Seed: var.seat (aisle / middle / back). Record: RECORD 06 (ticket stub + one line of script).
# Haruta stays polite until his Memory Room (see SPEECH SHIFT comment in haruta_room).

=== haruta_intro
@show haruta neutral at=c
@cam closeup haruta
> Haruta is at the corkboard by the front desk, reading the old flyers the way other people read museum labels. Hands behind his back. Touching nothing.
> Guitar lessons. A lost cat — found, says a second note pinned underneath. And a lavender flyer, curling at the corners: FRIDAY FILM CLUB. LOUNGE. BRING A CUSHION.
haruta[surprised]: Ah — sorry. Am I in your way?
> He steps aside half a pace, although there is plenty of room.
haruta[smile]: I was reading about the film club. They used to show films in the lounge on Fridays. On a real projector, it says. Sixteen millimetre.
haruta[thinking]: I would have liked to go. I think.
@show justin laugh at=r
justin: He *thinks*. Listen to him.
> Justin arrives the way he does everything — slightly too fast, then stopping exactly where he meant to. He hooks an arm over Haruta's shoulder.
justin: Ask him what he wants to do tonight. Go on. I have a bet running.
* [playful] "What do you want to do tonight?"
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[smile]: Anything is fine.
    justin[laugh]: Every time! Three years and I have never lost this bet.
* [careful] "He doesn't have to answer that."
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[smile]: Thank you. It's all right. Anything is fine.
    justin[laugh]: See? He answered anyway. He answers without answering. It's a skill.
> Haruta takes the teasing with the calm of someone who has been teased in two languages for years and has decided both are affectionate.
> Justin says something to him in Japanese, quick and fond. Haruta answers with one word. Justin laughs and lets go.
justin[smile]: I told him it's a good trait. It's a terrible trait. I'm getting chips.
@hide justin
haruta[smile]: He isn't wrong. About either part.
haruta: Please — sit, if you like. Anywhere is—
> He stops. Glances at the sofas, the chairs, the one stool behind the front desk. For a second, curiosity gets ahead of his manners.
haruta[thinking]: Actually — may I ask you something strange? When you go to the cinema, where do you sit?
haruta: People are very loyal to their seats. I've always wanted to know why.
* [careful] "On the aisle. So I can leave if I need to."
    @var seat = "aisle"
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[thinking]: So you can leave. {p} That's a very practical answer.
    haruta[smile]: Most people tell me it's for their legs.
* [sincere] "Right in the middle. So the screen is everything."
    @var seat = "middle"
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[smile]: The seat the screen was built for. The projectionists would approve.
* [quiet] "The back row. I like watching everyone watch."
    @var seat = "back"
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[surprised]: You watch the audience? {p} That's the best film in the building, sometimes.
you: And you?
haruta[smile]: Wherever the ticket says.
> He says it lightly, like a joke. It lands a little heavier than a joke should.
> From across the lobby, Justin's voice: *Haruta! Salt or seaweed?*
haruta: Anything is fine!
> *That is not a flavour!*
haruta[laugh]: …I think I sent something to this archive, you know. A ticket. Half of one. The part they give back to you.
haruta[thinking]: I can't remember why I thought the future would want it.
haruta[smile]: Thank you for asking me something. People usually just tell me the plan.
@hide haruta
@hub act1

=== haruta_intro_repeat
@show haruta smile at=c
haruta: The film club showed a different film every Friday. Somebody else always chose.
haruta[thinking]: That sounds very relaxing to me. I'm not sure it should.
@hide haruta
@hub act1

=== haruta_door
@bg studio_hall t=fade d=1.2
@amb room
@show haruta neutral at=c
> Door six doesn't match the others. Instead of plain glass, it has a small brass box-office window, and a slot underneath just wide enough for a ticket.
> Behind the glass, little letters flip over on a board, the way old cinema signs did. RECORD 06. NOW SHOWING.
> Haruta is standing in front of it with his hands in his cardigan pockets, like someone waiting for a friend who is running late.
haruta[smile]: Ah. Good evening. Again.
haruta: I've been standing here for a while. Deciding whether I want to go in.
haruta[thinking]: I'm not very practised at that part. Usually someone else decides, and I say it's fine.
@textbox echo
echo: RECORD 06 requires the owner's consent.
echo: The owner has been at this door for eleven minutes. I have not rushed him. I am learning not to rush people.
@textbox normal
haruta[smile]: Thank you, ECHO. That's very kind.
> He rests two fingers on the brass slot, as if checking whether it's warm.
haruta: I think I want to. I'm fairly sure. That's new for me.
haruta: Would you come in with me? You don't have to. If you'd rather not, that's f—
> He stops himself. Visibly. Like catching a cup before it tips.
haruta[shy]: …I would like it if you came. That's what I meant to say.
* "Open it."
    haruta[smile]: Then — after you. No. Together. The door is wide enough.
    @sfx door
    @goto haruta_room
* "Not yet."
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[smile]: That's fine.
    haruta[laugh]: Ah — I know. I say it constantly. But this time I checked first. I mean it.
    haruta: The film isn't going anywhere. That's the nice thing about films.
    @hide haruta
    @hub act2

=== haruta_room
@bg mem_cinema t=memory d=1.8
@music haruta
@amb cinema
@fx film n=1
@sfx projector
@cam reveal
> The hallway is gone. In its place: a small cinema, the kind that used to hide above shops.
> Fifty red seats, worn pink at the armrests. A curtain the colour of old wine. A clock over the exit that stopped at some point, and nobody minded.
> The projector is already running. Its beam cuts the dark overhead, full of slow dust — and loose film frames, drifting through the light like leaves that forgot to land.
@textbox echo
echo: RECORD 06 — OWNER: HARUTA. TYPE: TICKET STUB (HALF), ONE LINE OF SCRIPT.
echo: DATE: OCTOBER, THREE YEARS AGO. SCREENING: 23:10. ATTENDANCE: 2.
echo: UNRESOLVED SUBJECTS: 1.
@textbox normal
@show haruta neutral at=c
haruta: The Dalbit. Upstairs from the key-cutting shop.
haruta[smile]: It even smells right. Yesterday's popcorn, and the radiator.
> He stands at the top of the aisle, hands in his pockets, looking at the room as if it might politely ask him to leave.
@if rooms() == 0
    haruta[thinking]: ECHO said there's one person it can't identify. In every record.
    haruta: I suppose we'll meet them. I hope they don't mind an audience.
@elif rooms() >= 4
    haruta[smile]: One person ECHO can't identify. I wonder who it could possibly be.
    > He looks at you, very politely, for slightly too long.
    haruta[laugh]: Sorry. I'm told that's a running joke in this building now.
@else
    haruta[thinking]: The others say there's always a figure of light. And that it's usually… well.
    haruta: Is it going to be you again? I only ask so I can prepare my face.
* [playful] "Do we need tickets?"
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[laugh]: I think we are the tickets, tonight.
    haruta: Please don't tell ECHO I said that. It will put it in a record.
* [sincere] "Tell me about this place."
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta: Old films, mostly. Late screenings on weekdays. The seats were cheaper than the popcorn.
    haruta[smile]: I came here a lot, my first autumn in Seoul. It was the one place where not understanding everything felt normal.
* [quiet] Wait at the top of the aisle until he's ready.
    @rel haruta trust+1
    > You don't say anything. After a moment he notices that you're waiting for him, and not the other way round. His shoulders come down half an inch.
    haruta: Thank you. I'm ready. I think.
haruta: Please — sit wherever you'd like. I'll just—
@sfx glitch
@fx glitch n=0.7
> The picture shivers like a tape being rewound. Colours bleed at the edges. Lines of static roll up the walls and carry the present away with them.
@hide all
@fx film n=1
@textbox memory
> Three autumns ago. A boy at the box-office window, reading the schedule taped to the glass very slowly.
> Haruta at seventeen. Neater than anyone needs to be at eleven at night. A phrasebook in his coat pocket, the spine cracked open at the same page.
> The ticket seller asks him something over her knitting, fast and kind. He catches two words. *Which.* *Film.*
> He has already chosen. He chose on the bus, from the list on his phone, by counting.
haruta: That one, please. *The Umbrella Shop.*
clerk: That one? It's very old, you know. Hardly anybody talks in it.
haruta: Yes. Please. One ticket.
> That is the reason. He doesn't have the Korean for it yet, so he only smiles. She tears the ticket in half and gives him the smaller piece.
> Inside, there is exactly one other person.
@if var.seat == "aisle"
    > A silhouette of light, on the aisle three rows from the back. One foot already out in the aisle, as if ready to leave.
@elif var.seat == "back"
    > A silhouette of light, alone in the very back row, where you can see the screen and the whole room at once.
@else
    > A silhouette of light, dead centre. The one seat in the house the screen was built for.
> Young Haruta sits exactly where his ticket says. Row F, seat 7. It doesn't occur to him to sit anywhere else.
@sfx projector
> The film runs. Black and white. A man who owns an umbrella shop lends umbrellas to strangers on rainy days, and nobody ever brings one back.
> Eleven lines of dialogue in ninety minutes. He will count them later. He understands nine.
> In the last scene it rains. The shop door opens, and someone comes in holding an umbrella out, handle first.
> Then the credits. The house lights come up a little, the way they do in places that trust you to leave on your own.
> Nobody leaves.
you: Excuse me. {p} Can I ask you something?
> The silhouette has turned in its seat. ECHO holds the voice carefully, like something it can't quite read the label on.
haruta: …Yes?
you: If you had chosen the film tonight. Which one would it have been?
> A simple question. He has the words for it. He's been learning them for weeks: *which*, *would*, *choose*.
> What he doesn't have is an answer. He's not sure he has ever had one.
haruta: Anything is fine.
haruta: …Sorry. That's not what you asked. I mean — I don't know.
you: That's okay.
you: You can think about it during the credits.
> So he does. So do they. Two people in a fifty-seat room, watching names go by that neither of them can read fast enough.
> The music ends. The screen goes white, then grey.
you: Did you decide?
haruta: Not yet.
you: Me neither. I just keep watching this one.
> The silhouette pulls on a coat and goes — up the aisle, through the curtain, gone. People don't say goodbye to strangers at the cinema. It's one of the rules.
> Young Haruta stays until the projector clicks off. By the exit, there is a flyer: FUTURE LETTER PROJECT — STUDIO 25 — SEND SOMETHING TO WHO YOU'LL BE.
> He writes one line on a card, in Japanese, very neatly, and clips it to his half of the ticket.
@textbox echo
echo: TRANSCRIPT: 「見知らぬ二人が、エンドロールまで残る。」
echo: TRANSLATION: "Two strangers stay for the credits."
@textbox normal
@sfx glitch
@fx glitch n=0.5
> The tape spools forward. The present comes back in stripes.
@fx film n=1
@show haruta thinking at=c
@cam closeup haruta
@if rooms() == 0
    > Haruta is still looking at the seat where the silhouette sat.
    haruta: In the lobby… I asked where you sit.
    @if var.seat == "aisle"
        haruta: You said the aisle. So you can leave.
    @elif var.seat == "back"
        haruta: You said the back row. So you can watch everyone watch.
    @else
        haruta: You said the middle. So the screen is everything.
    > He turns, slowly, and looks at you the way you look at a word you've only ever seen written down.
    haruta[surprised]: That was you.
@else
    haruta[smile]: It's you again. Isn't it.
    haruta: I knew when they sat down. You told me in the lobby where you sit. I've been waiting all night to see if you'd sit there.
    haruta[laugh]: You did. Of course you did.
> And there it is, surfacing like a picture in a developing tray.
> A cinema above a key-cutting shop. A woman knitting in the box office.
> A film you had already seen in three cities — dubbed once, subtitled twice. The same every time, because hardly anyone talks.
> A boy in row F who said *anything is fine* and then apologised to you for it.
* [sincere] "It was me. I remember the umbrella shop."
    @rel haruta trust+1 closeness+1
    haruta[smile]: The umbrella, handle first. I think about that shot more than is reasonable.
* [playful] "In my defence, you never answered."
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[laugh]: I know. I've had three years. You would think I'd have prepared something.
* [quiet] Walk down the aisle and sit where the silhouette sat.
    @rel haruta trust+1 closeness+1
    > The seat is warm, the way reconstructed things are warm. From here you can see him, standing where he stood.
    haruta[shy]: …Yes. Just like that. That's exactly how it looked.
> He walks down to row F and sits in seat 7. Where the ticket said. He notices himself doing it, and laughs under his breath.
haruta: Why did you choose it? *The Umbrella Shop.* Nobody chooses it.
you: We moved a lot. Every city had a different everything.
you: That film was the same in all of them. Nobody talks, so nothing gets lost.
haruta[thinking]: …I chose it because it had the fewest words on the schedule. I counted, on the bus.
haruta: My Korean was very new. That film didn't need me to understand it.
haruta[smile]: So you chose it because it didn't change. And I chose it because it didn't ask anything of me.
haruta: Two people hiding in the same film. From different things.
> Above you, a frame drifts through the beam: the umbrella, handle first, turning slowly like it's looking for someone to hand itself to.
haruta: Do you know, "anything is fine" was one of the first things I learned to say properly?
haruta: Before my own address. I practised it on the plane.
haruta[smile]: It's a very good phrase. It's polite. It's short. And nobody ever asks a follow-up question.
haruta[thinking]: That's the trouble. Nobody ever asks a follow-up question.
haruta: It worked so well, I kept using it after I understood the questions. Menus. Taxis. Group dinners. Justin's bets.
haruta: Everyone thinks I'm easy-going. I am, mostly. I like it when people are happy with the plan.
haruta: But sometimes I say "anything is fine," and then I go home and write a whole scene about what I actually wanted.
you: A scene?
haruta[shy]: In my phone. Little ones. Justin turning in the rain. Hamin handing someone a drink they didn't ask for.
haruta[laugh]: I have eleven scenes of Daniel losing to a claw machine. They're all the same scene. That's what makes them good.
haruta: I'm very good at knowing what a scene needs. I'm less good at knowing what I need in it.
* [sincere] "So — what would you have chosen?"
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[thinking]: I've made lists. Honestly. Three years of lists.
    haruta: Every time, I cross out everything until only *The Umbrella Shop* is left. And that one doesn't count. I only picked it because it was easy.
* [playful] "Three years is a very long credit sequence."
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[laugh]: The longest in history. Someone should give it an award.
    haruta[smile]: Best Pause. I'd accept it politely and say anything is fine.
* [quiet] Don't ask. Let the frames drift.
    @rel haruta trust+1
    > The projector ticks. A frame of rain floats past, then a frame of the shop door. He watches them go, and you watch him watch them.
    haruta: You're doing it again. Giving me the credits to think in.
* [romantic] "You don't have to be easy-going with me." {if haruta.trust >= 2}
    @rel haruta romance+1 trust+1
    > He looks at you for a long moment. The polite half-smile he wears everywhere slips, just slightly, like a picture going out of focus.
    haruta: …That might be the kindest thing anyone has said to me. In any language.
haruta: You know, you're the only person who ever heard "anything is fine" and didn't take it as an answer.
haruta: You gave me time instead. Four minutes of credits. I've been thinking about it ever since.
haruta[thinking]: Can I tell you something? I've been talking to you very carefully all night.
haruta: Like a guest. Like someone who might be asked to leave if he says the wrong thing.
haruta: I think I can stop being careful now.
# SPEECH SHIFT: casual from here (ko 반말 / ja plain)
haruta[shy]: {{playerName}}.
> Just your name. Nothing softening it in front, no politeness after. He says it the way you test whether a floor will hold.
haruta[laugh]: There. The building didn't fall down.
* [playful] "Daniel said he'd wear you down by three a.m."
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[laugh]: Daniel doesn't get credit for this. He can have a special thanks.
    haruta: Small font. Near the end. After the catering.
* [sincere] "Say it again."
    @rel haruta romance+1
    haruta[shy]: {{playerName}}.
    haruta: …Okay. That's enough. My face is doing something and I didn't approve it.
* [quiet] Smile, and let him have the moment.
    @rel haruta trust+1
    > He sees the smile. He doesn't comment on it. He just sits back in seat 7 like it finally fits.
haruta[smile]: Hey. ECHO. Can you run the credits again? Just the credits.
@textbox echo
echo: Running credits. Duration: four minutes, twelve seconds.
echo: I have always liked this part. Nobody watches it.
@textbox normal
haruta: We did.
@sfx projector
> The screen fills with slow white names. The house lights dim to that halfway glow that means *stay if you like*.
* [romantic] Sit down in F8, right beside him. {if haruta.closeness >= 2 or haruta.romance >= 1}
    @rel haruta romance+1 closeness+1
    > F8. The armrest between you has been worn pale by thirty years of elbows. He lifts his off it, so you can have it.
    > Then, after a second, he puts it back. Next to yours.
    haruta[shy]: I was going to give you the armrest. Then I thought — no. I'd like half.
* [romantic] Rest your hand on the armrest between you, palm up. Let him decide. {if haruta.romance >= 1}
    @rel haruta romance+1 trust+1
    > He looks at your hand for a long time, as if it's a question he actually has to answer.
    > Then he hooks one finger, carefully, around one of yours. Just one.
    haruta[shy]: I decided.
* [quiet] Stay a seat apart and watch the names go by.
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[smile]: Two people, one empty seat between them. It's a better composition, honestly.
    haruta: The empty seat makes you notice them.
> The names scroll. Neither of you can read them fast enough. It doesn't matter at all.
haruta: At home in Hyōgo, the cinema near the station had seats that snapped up so hard they clapped.
haruta[laugh]: Every film ended in applause. Even the bad ones. Especially the bad ones — everyone left at once.
haruta: Here I learned to stay. I didn't know that was something I'd learned until tonight.
> The music ends. The screen goes white, then grey. Exactly as before.
* [playful] "Did you decide?"
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[laugh]: Not yet.
    haruta[smile]: But I'm going to actually look this time. That's the difference. Last time I was just waiting for it to go away.
* [sincere] "You don't have to know yet. Just don't say 'anything' when you don't mean it."
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[thinking]: That's fair. I'll try.
    haruta[smile]: If I say it, you can look at me like that. Like you're doing right now.
* [romantic] "I'll wait through the credits as many times as you need." {if haruta.romance >= 2}
    @rel haruta romance+1
    haruta[shy]: That's… a lot of credits.
    haruta: I'll try to decide before you've memorised the catering department.
haruta: ECHO. May I change my record? Just one word.
@textbox echo
echo: The owner may amend the record.
echo: I will keep the original. I do not like deleting things.
@textbox normal
> A card drifts down out of the beam and settles in his hand: his own handwriting, three years younger. He runs his thumb under the first word.
haruta: This word — *mishiranu*. "Strangers." Or, "people who don't know each other."
haruta: Take it out, please.
@textbox echo
echo: Amended: 「二人が、エンドロールまで残る。」
echo: TRANSLATION: "Two people stay for the credits." {p} I am not sure this is correct.
@textbox normal
haruta[smile]: It isn't, quite. In English it only gets shorter.
haruta: In Japanese, when you just say "two people" like that… it can mean a pair. The two of us.
haruta[shy]: I didn't have to add anything. I only had to take one word out.
@textbox echo
echo: RECORD 06 — UNRESOLVED SUBJECT: RESOLVED. IDENTIFICATION: RECIPIENT {{playerName}}.
echo: ATTENDANCE: 2. UNCHANGED.
@textbox normal
> The projector winds down. The frames settle out of the air like snow deciding to stop.
> Haruta stands, and waits at the end of the row until you stand too. Then you walk up the aisle side by side.
@flag room_haruta
@unlock memory:haruta
@autosave
@goto hub_after_room

=== haruta_hub
@if flag.room_haruta and not flag.haruta_postchat
    @show haruta smile at=c
    @show daniel surprised at=r
    daniel: Wait. Wait wait wait. Haruta. Say that again.
    haruta: I said, move your feet, you're on my cardigan.
    daniel: No "please"! No "sorry"! He's talking *comfortably!* To *us!*
    daniel[laugh]: I said I'd wear him down by three a.m.! What time is it? Somebody tell me it's three.
    haruta[smile]: It wasn't you, Daniel. Sorry.
    daniel: Special thanks, at least?
    haruta: Small font. After the catering.
    @hide daniel
    @show hyunjun smile at=r
    hyunjun: Okay, okay, tie-breaker! Tteokbokki or convenience store? Haruta, you decide, you're neutral.
    haruta: Anything is—
    > He stops. Looks at you. Looks back at Hyunjun.
    haruta: Convenience store. The one on the corner, not the one by the station. The egg sandwiches are better.
    haruta: And I want strawberry milk.
    hyunjun[surprised]: He has a *specific store.* He has a *specific milk.* Who are you?
    hyunjun[laugh]: I love this. I'm writing it down. Corner store! Everyone!
    @hide hyunjun
    > Hyunjun runs off to spread the news. Haruta watches him go, a little pink at the ears.
    haruta[shy]: That was terrifying. My hands are cold.
    haruta: I've wanted those egg sandwiches for a year and a half. I just kept saying "anything."
    * [playful] "Egg sandwich. A bold first choice."
        @rel haruta closeness+1
        haruta[laugh]: I'm starting with low stakes. Next week I'm choosing a whole restaurant.
        haruta: Justin's going to cry.
    * [sincere] "How did it feel?"
        @rel haruta trust+1
        haruta[thinking]: Like walking out on stage before anyone says your name.
        haruta[smile]: …And then nothing bad happens. Hyunjun just gets excited. He was always going to get excited.
    * [romantic] "Get me one too? I trust your taste now." {if haruta.romance >= 1}
        @rel haruta romance+1 closeness+1
        haruta[surprised]: My taste?
        haruta[shy]: I was going to buy you one anyway. I decided in the room. I just hadn't found the moment to say it.
    haruta: Thank you for coming in with me, {{playerName}}. I'm going to keep saying your name like that. I've decided.
    @hide haruta
    @flag haruta_postchat
@elif flag.room_haruta
    @show haruta smile at=c
    @if rooms() >= 6
        haruta: We're near the end of the reel now. It's funny — I'm not in a hurry at all.
        haruta[thinking]: I usually am, at the end. I want to be out before the lights come up.
    @else
        haruta: I'm practising. Justin asked me salt or seaweed. I said seaweed. I didn't even like it that much.
        haruta[laugh]: It's the principle.
    @hide haruta
@else
    @show haruta smile at=c
    @show charlie smile at=l
    charlie: Haruta-hyung, you went up to look at your door, right? Is it scary?
    haruta: Not scary. It has a little box-office window. Very well designed.
    charlie: And?
    haruta[smile]: And I came back down again.
    charlie[laugh]: That's the most Haruta-hyung thing that's ever happened.
    @hide charlie
    haruta: I'll go in. I would like to, I think. I just want to be sure it's me who decides.
    haruta[thinking]: Is that strange? It feels strange, saying it.
    @hide haruta
@hub act2

=== haruta_route
@chapter route
@time 03:50
@var route = "haruta"
@bg studio_lounge t=fade d=1.4
@music quiet
@amb room
@cam reveal
> Seventy minutes left. The others scatter through the building in twos and threes, the way people do when a party is almost over and nobody wants to say so.
> You don't have to think about where to go. You follow a sound from the ground floor: a small, patient ticking, like a clock that has somewhere to be.
> The lounge is dark except for a white rectangle on the far wall.
> Behind it, on a tower of stacked chairs, an old sixteen-millimetre projector whirrs and ticks and smells of hot dust.
> Haruta is crouched beside it, sleeves pushed up, an empty spool in each hand, like someone halfway through a magic trick he hasn't practised.
@show haruta smile at=c
haruta: You found me. And I found the film club.
haruta: It was in the cupboard under the stairs, where the flyer said. Hanbi found the extension cord. Hanbi always has the extension cord.
> There's no film in it. The projector throws pure light at the wall — an empty frame, very bright, very patient.
haruta[thinking]: I found the projector. I didn't find anything to put in it.
haruta: So it's showing nothing. I've been watching it for twenty minutes. It's surprisingly good.
* [playful] "Five stars. No notes."
    @rel haruta closeness+1
    haruta[laugh]: The pacing sags a bit in the middle. But the lighting is incredible.
* [sincere] "Are you okay? After everything tonight?"
    @rel haruta trust+1
    haruta[thinking]: I think so. It's a lot of feeling for one night.
    haruta[smile]: I'm used to about one feeling a week. Maximum. Scheduled.
* [quiet] Sit down on the floor beside him, in front of the empty light.
    @rel haruta closeness+1 romance+1
    > He shifts over without looking, making room, the way you do for someone you already expected.
    haruta[smile]: Good seat. Best in the house.
> For a while you both look at the blank frame. Dust turns in the beam, slow as snow in a paperweight.
haruta: I've written four scenes tonight. That's a record. Even for me.
